component SigilLanguage {
  goal {
    Define the versioned source and interpretation contract for Sigil documents.

    Give deterministic tools one structural model for components, expansions,
    sections, concepts, imports, and recoverable diagnostics.
  }

  interface {
    LanguageVersion {
      sigilVersion = "0.7.0"

      workspace configuration declares the LanguageVersion that governs
      interpretation
    }

    SourceDocument {
      accepts UTF-8 .sigil source containing imports, components, expands, sections,
      concept blocks, semantic units, and attached literal blocks

      interprets structural forms through SourceGrammar and malformed input through
      SourceRecovery

      preserves recovered declarations, semantic units, literal blocks, concept
      identities, original physical lines, and source ranges

      returns structural diagnostics and the deterministic partial document defined
      by SourceRecovery

      treats diagnostic message prose as explanatory and non-normative while code,
      severity, ordering, file path, range, retained result, and resume point carry
      the compatibility contract
    }

    SourceGrammar {
      recognizes structure only when a complete trimmed physical line matches the
      applicable production at a top-level, form, section, or concept boundary

      accepts this normative structural grammar where horizontal whitespace is space
      or tab, newline is LF or CRLF, name is [A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_]*, concept-id is
      [A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_-]*, path-token is one or more non-whitespace characters
      other than braces or comma, backticks is three or more backtick characters, and
      physical-line is any sequence of characters other than newline:
      ```ebnf
      document       = { blank | import | component | expand } ;
      import         = "@" path-token ws "import" ws "{" ws-opt name-list ws-opt "}" line-end ;
      name-list      = name { ws-opt "," ws-opt name } ;
      component      = "component" ws name ws-opt "{" line-end { section | blank } "}" line-end ;
      expand         = "expand" ws name ws-opt "{" line-end { section | blank } "}" line-end ;
      section        = section-name ws-opt "{" line-end section-body "}" line-end ;
      section-name   = "goal" | "interface" | "state" | "logic" | "constraints" | "decisions" | "cases" ;
      section-body   = { blank | concept-block | prose-unit } ;
      concept-block  = concept-header { blank | prose-unit } "}" line-end ;
      concept-header = header-text ws-opt "{" line-end ;
      section-header = name ws-opt "{" line-end ;
      prose-unit     = prose-line { newline prose-line } [ newline literal-block ] line-end ;
      literal-block  = opening-fence newline { literal-line newline } closing-fence line-end ;
      opening-fence  = backticks [ fence-info ] ;
      closing-fence  = backticks ;
      fence-info     = physical-line ;
      literal-line   = physical-line ;
      prose-line     = physical-line ;
      blank          = ws-opt newline ;
      ws             = ( " " | "\t" ) { " " | "\t" } ;
      ws-opt         = { " " | "\t" } ;
      line-end       = newline | end-of-file ;
      newline        = "\n" | "\r\n" ;
      ```

      after trimming, header-text is nonempty, does not start with @, and contains
      none of =:()[]<>"' or backtick; its complete value is the supplied concept
      identifier and is valid only when it matches concept-id

      fence-info is the complete suffix after the opening backticks; its trimmed
      nonempty value is the supplied literal type and is valid only when it matches
      [A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_+.-]*

      directly inside a form, any section-header whose name is outside section-name
      is an unknown section header; every other nonblank line that is neither a
      structural close nor a valid section is unexpected form content

      indentation before structural forms is insignificant, while indentation and
      characters inside prose and literal bodies follow their own preservation rules

      inside a section or concept, an opening fence consumes the maximal contiguous
      run of three or more leading backticks before interpreting its suffix as
      fence-info and takes precedence over blank-line, closing-brace, concept-header,
      and prose recognition; its closing fence contains only backticks, has at least
      the opener's length after trimming, and takes precedence over literal-line

      outside a literal, a closing brace takes precedence over concept-header and
      prose recognition when brace depth permits closing the current concept,
      section, or form

      a concept header takes precedence over prose only at a paragraph boundary and
      at the current section or concept's baseline brace depth; every other nonblank
      line inside a section is prose-line

      after structural recognition, scan every ordinary prose line from left to right
      and add one for each { and subtract one for each }; quotes and apparent comment
      or annotation text do not suppress braces, while literal bodies are inert

      a concept begins at brace depth one and free section prose at depth zero; the
      net line delta controls recognition on later lines, an unmatched } may lower
      depth, later { may restore it, and any depth at EOF follows SourceRecovery
      finalization

      Sigil defines no comment or annotation syntax; such text is ordinary prose
      inside a section and unexpected content at top level or directly inside a form
    }

    SourceRecovery {
      recovers malformed source according to this deterministic matrix, emitting
      diagnostics in source-scan order followed by required-section diagnostics:
      ```text
      Condition                         Diagnostic                         Severity Retained result and resume point
      unsupported LanguageVersion       SIGIL_UNSUPPORTED_VERSION         error    empty document; stop interpretation
      malformed or unexpected top level SIGIL_PARSE_STRUCTURE             error    skip that physical line; resume at top level
      unknown section header            SIGIL_UNKNOWN_SECTION             error    skip the header; remain directly inside the form
      unexpected content inside form    SIGIL_PARSE_STRUCTURE             error    skip that physical line; remain inside the form
      missing goal                       SIGIL_MISSING_GOAL                error    retain the component after the source scan
      missing interface                  SIGIL_MISSING_INTERFACE           error    retain the component after the source scan
      invalid concept identifier         SIGIL_INVALID_CONCEPT_IDENTIFIER error    retain the concept block and its content
      empty concept block                SIGIL_EMPTY_CONCEPT_BLOCK         error    retain the empty block
      nested concept header              SIGIL_NESTED_CONCEPT_BLOCK        error    retain it as outer-concept prose with brace tracking
      unclosed concept at EOF            SIGIL_PARSE_STRUCTURE             error    finalize concept at EOF, then enclosing section and form
      unclosed section at EOF            SIGIL_PARSE_STRUCTURE             error    finalize section at EOF, then enclosing form
      unclosed component or expand       SIGIL_PARSE_STRUCTURE             error    finalize the declaration at EOF
      detached literal block             SIGIL_DETACHED_LITERAL_BLOCK      error    retain the literal in a recovered semantic unit
      literal without introduction       SIGIL_LITERAL_WITHOUT_INTRODUCTION error   retain the literal in a recovered semantic unit
      invalid literal type               SIGIL_INVALID_LITERAL_TYPE        error    retain the literal and supplied type
      unclosed literal at EOF             SIGIL_UNCLOSED_LITERAL_BLOCK      error    retain body through EOF, then finalize enclosing forms
      ungrouped interface region          SIGIL_MISSING_CONCEPT_IDENTIFIER warning  retain every unit; resume at the region boundary
      ```

      after source-scan diagnostics, required-section diagnostics follow component
      declaration order and emit missing goal before missing interface for each
      component

      every retained declaration, section, concept, semantic unit, literal block, and
      diagnostic preserves its deterministic source range

      unsupported-version diagnostics use the zero-width start of the source;
      skipped-line, unknown-section, invalid-identifier, nested-header, and literal
      diagnostics span the complete nonblank physical line; empty-concept diagnostics
      span the recovered concept and missing-section diagnostics span the component

      unclosed concept, section, component, or expand diagnostics use a zero-width
      range at column one of that construct's opening line; an unclosed literal
      diagnostic spans its opening-fence line

      a contiguous ungrouped interface region begins with the first free semantic
      unit after a section open, concept close, or prior region boundary and ends
      before a blank line, concept header, section close, or EOF; its warning spans
      from the first retained unit start through the last retained unit end and is
      emitted when that boundary is reached

      a detached or unintroduced literal creates one semantic unit under the current
      component or expand, section, and concept when present, with empty normalized
      prose and physical prose lines, the literal as its sole attachment, and the
      literal's complete range; each contiguous malformed fence creates a separate
      unit

      source locations use one-based lines and one-based UTF-16 code-unit columns;
      ranges are start-inclusive and end-exclusive, exclude newline delimiters, treat
      CRLF as one newline, and place EOF after the final code unit or at column one
      of the trailing empty line; LSP UTF-16 positions subtract one from both axes
    }

    ComponentContract {
      component defines the reusable public contract of one coherent system part
      through its goal and interface

      expand contributes collective operational detail without changing or overriding
      the public contract

      goal publicly states the component's responsibility, boundary, and intended
      outcome

      interface exposes the operations, data, events, results, failures, and
      observable promises available to dependents
    }

    ConceptIdentity {
      a concept block groups one or more related semantic units under a reusable
      identifier

      a concept is public when it occurs in interface and otherwise remains private
      to its component and matching expands

      a component and all matching expands share one flat concept namespace

      repeated blocks with the same resolved identifier contribute collectively
      without overriding one another

      imported public concepts retain their originating identity only when reused in
      matching expands
    }

    ImportSemantics {
      import is the sole declaration of dependency on another Sigil component

      an expand declared in another source depends on its matching component through
      an explicit named import

      a named import exposes the imported component's public goal, interface, and
      public concept identities

      imported operational detail and private concepts remain unavailable to
      dependents

      every resolved imported name is used by a qualifying contract, operational,
      structural, or directory-surface reference
    }

    SectionSemantics {
      goal publicly describes why the component exists, the responsibility it owns,
      and its intended outcome

      interface describes operations, data, events, results, failures, and observable
      promises available to dependents

      state describes meaningful runtime or domain data, configurations, modes, and
      conditions that exist or change during execution

      logic describes behavior, flows, algorithms, transformations, decisions, and
      lifecycle transitions

      constraints describe rules, policies, invariants, architecture decisions, and
      binding technology choices

      decisions describes the durable rationale for a chosen course, including scope,
      assumptions, trade-offs, design issues addressed, discarded alternatives,
      consequences, and revisit conditions

      cases describe representative outcomes, examples, edge cases, and observable
      acceptance scenarios
    }

    SemanticUnitDiscipline {
      each blank-line-delimited prose paragraph expresses one distinct idea

      physical wrapping inside one paragraph does not change semantic identity

      blank lines terminate semantic units and do not themselves create semantic
      units
    }

    LiteralBlock {
      three or more backticks open a literal block and may be followed by one
      optional type token matching [A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_+.-]*

      a closing fence contains at least as many backticks as its opener and no other
      content

      an opening fence immediately follows its introducing prose without an
      intervening blank line

      literal bodies preserve blank lines, braces, apparent Sigil syntax, and
      relative indentation as uninterpreted content
    }

    FormattingStyle {
      ordinary prose uses at most 79 content characters per physical line without
      counting leading indentation

      structural lines, fence delimiters, and literal bodies are excluded from the
      prose-width limit
    }
  }
}

expand SigilLanguage {
  constraints {
    LanguageVersion {
      LanguageVersion is the sole owner of the Sigil language and
      workspace-configuration version literal.

      Tools interpret only the exact configured LanguageVersion they support and
      reject earlier or later version literals without applying semantic-version
      ranges.

      During pre-production 0.y.z development, breaking interpretation changes
      increment the minor version and reset the patch version.

      After 1.0.0, breaking interpretation changes require a new major version.

      Before and after 1.0.0, compatible syntax or semantic functionality increments
      the minor version and resets patch; an interpretation-preserving behavioral
      clarification or defect correction increments patch; a clarification that
      changes any accepted result follows the applicable breaking-change rule;
      interpretation-neutral editorial changes do not change LanguageVersion.
    }

    SourceDocument {
      Every component contains goal and interface sections.

      Ungrouped interface content remains parseable and produces one warning for each
      contiguous ungrouped region.

      SourceGrammar is the normative lexical and structural grammar.

      SourceRecovery defines the diagnostic, retained result, and resume boundary for
      every malformed structural form.

      Recovery never invents a declaration or silently reinterprets skipped content.
    }

    ComponentContract {
      Component dependencies are declared through imports and are not repeated as
      interface dependency lines.

      Matching expands add detail collectively and never override the component
      declaration or another expand.

      A dependent receives the imported component's public contract without its
      state, logic, constraints, decisions, cases, or private concepts.

      Implementation-hiding rules belong in constraints unless they define an
      observable promise.
    }

    ConceptIdentity {
      Concept blocks are flat and cannot nest.

      Every concept block contains at least one semantic unit.

      Concept identifiers match [A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_-]* and contain no spaces.

      PascalCase without hyphens or underscores is preferred but not required for
      validity.

      Concept references are case-sensitive.

      Accessible identifiers are unique without regard to case across the transitive
      import graph.

      Sigil provides no dotted concept notation, qualification, aliases, or local
      shadowing.

      Reusing an imported concept in a matching expand preserves its originating
      identity while keeping importer semantic units contextual to the importer.

      A same-named concept declared on the importing component remains a distinct
      local identity and is ambiguous with the imported identity.

      Each case-insensitive identifier group containing more than one distinct
      accessible identity emits one error SIGIL_AMBIGUOUS_CONCEPT_IDENTIFIER per
      component context in accessible-identifier discovery order, ranged over that
      component declaration; all identities remain in the namespace and textual
      occurrences of that identifier remain unbound.

      Reusing an imported concept in a matching expand interface re-exposes the same
      identity to downstream importers.

      Provider context never includes semantic units contributed by consumers.

      An exact-case accessible identifier in semantic content resolves as a reference
      only when the immediately preceding and following characters, when present, are
      outside ASCII A-Z, a-z, 0-9, underscore, and hyphen.

      Unmatched words remain ordinary semantic content without unresolved-concept
      diagnostics.

      Concept identities provide grouping, reuse, navigation, and highlighting but
      define no source-anchor behavior.
    }

    ImportSemantics {
      Ordinary file imports resolve against the named .sigil source.

      Directory imports follow the provider-owned DirectoryImportSurface contract;
      SigilLanguage owns only the general import and use rules.

      An expand declared in the same source as its component matches locally without
      an import.

      An expand declared in another source matches its component only through a
      resolved named import in the expand's source.

      A cross-file expand without that import produces SIGIL_EXPAND_WITHOUT_COMPONENT
      and contributes no collected expansion or concept-namespace content.

      A structural import used by an expand does not project the component back into
      its own concept namespace as an imported dependency.

      Imported names must resolve to components exposed by the selected source or
      directory-import surface.

      Component declaration identity, uniqueness, imported-name resolution, matching
      expand attachment, and textual component references compare names by exact
      case; names differing only by case are distinct.

      An unresolved path retains an import result with no resolved names; an
      unresolved name retains every independently resolved name from the same import;
      an import cycle retains resolved import edges and names, and resolution of
      declarations outside the cycle continues.

      Every unresolved path emits error SIGIL_UNRESOLVED_IMPORT_PATH over its
      complete import line in source-discovery and import-declaration order; every
      unresolved name then emits error SIGIL_UNRESOLVED_IMPORTED_COMPONENT over its
      complete import line in resolved-import and written-name order.

      Import cycles are then discovered depth first in source-discovery and
      import-edge order; every edge returning to an active source emits error
      SIGIL_IMPORT_CYCLE over that closing import declaration, after all
      unresolved-name diagnostics.

      Import paths resolve from the selected workspace root, not the declaring
      source; backslash becomes slash, repeated slash and . segments collapse, and
      each .. removes one preceding root-relative segment before lookup; a leading or
      excess .. remains an outside-workspace traversal and is unresolved.

      A normalized path ending in .sigil selects that exact file and every other path
      delegates target selection to DirectoryImportSurface; matching is
      case-sensitive, equivalent normalized paths select one target, and a target
      outside the selected workspace is unresolved rather than imported.

      Import use is evaluated separately for each resolved imported name in its
      declaring source document.

      Exact-case component references and imported public-concept references in
      interface, state, logic, constraints, or cases count as use.

      Component references use the same immediately adjacent ASCII A-Z, a-z, 0-9,
      underscore, and hyphen boundary exclusion as concept references.

      An applicable local expand and intentional provider directory-surface exposure
      count as structural use.

      References in goal, decisions, literal bodies, other source documents,
      differently cased words, and identifier substrings do not count as use;
      comment-shaped or annotation-shaped ordinary prose follows its containing
      section because Sigil defines no separate syntax for it.

      An unresolved or ambiguous imported name does not additionally produce an
      unused-import diagnostic.

      Repeating a name in one import retains separate ordered name entries, each with
      independent resolution and use; it does not duplicate a resolved public
      identity.

      Every qualifying textual or structural use is attributed to all repeated
      entries that resolve to the same name and provider; with no qualifying use,
      every repeated entry independently emits SIGIL_UNUSED_IMPORT in written order.
    }

    DeclarationMultiplicity {
      Component names are unique across the selected workspace; every declaration of
      a duplicated name is retained and receives error SIGIL_DUPLICATE_COMPONENT in
      source-discovery order, while the name binds no import, expand, or concept
      context.

      Repeated sections in one component or expand are allowed ordered contributions
      to the same section semantics; any goal or interface occurrence satisfies that
      component's required-section presence.
    }

    SectionSemantics {
      goal and interface define the public component contract.

      State may describe meaningful data that exists or changes as part of runtime or
      domain semantics; storage layout remains implementation detail unless the
      layout itself carries domain meaning.

      decisions is an optional expand section.

      decisions content is free-form and does not require concept blocks, labeled
      fields, or a complete rationale schema.

      decisions records concise conclusions and supporting rationale rather than
      prompts, session transcripts, or hidden reasoning.

      When present, decision scope states the boundary where a chosen course applies
      and its important exclusions without attempting to enumerate every current
      dependent.

      A binding outcome remains in constraints; decisions explains why that outcome
      was selected.
    }

    SemanticUnitDiscipline {
      Distinct prose-level ideas are separated by blank lines in every section.

      Adjacent wrapped prose lines normalize to one semantic unit with one source
      range and retained original physical lines.

      A literal block attaches to the immediately preceding prose semantic unit.
    }

    LiteralBlock {
      A blank line between introducing prose and its opening fence is invalid.

      A literal block without preceding prose in the same section or concept block is
      invalid.

      Structural indentation equal to the opening-fence indentation is removed from
      literal-body lines when present while additional indentation is preserved.

      Literal bodies create no component, concept, import, glossary, ownership, or
      other semantic references.

      Literal bodies are not prose-wrapped or width-checked.
    }

    FormattingStyle {
      Prose width is measured in Unicode code points after leading indentation.

      Canonical wrapping treats exactly U+0009 through U+000D, U+0020, U+00A0,
      U+1680, U+2000 through U+200A, U+2028, U+2029, U+202F, U+205F, U+3000, and
      U+FEFF as whitespace, collapses each run to one U+0020 SPACE, and never changes
      semantic identity; tabs and nonbreaking spaces therefore become ordinary
      spaces.

      An indivisible prose token longer than 79 content characters produces error
      SIGIL_UNFORMATTABLE_LINE over its complete physical line; parsing retains the
      line, formatting returns no writable result, and check fails.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    LanguageVersion {
      Decision: Require an explicit configured LanguageVersion and use semantic
      version increments to signal interpretation compatibility.

      Scope: Governs Sigil source and workspace-configuration interpretation;
      excludes CLI, core, LSP, skill, and editor artifact versions.

      Trade-offs: Pre-production breaking changes consume minor versions instead of
      treating every 0.y.z change as equivalent.

      Consequences: Tools reject every non-exact configured version and incompatible
      changes require an explicit migration.

      Revisit when: LanguageVersion reaches 1.0.0 or the compatibility policy
      changes.
    }

    SourceDocument {
      Decision: Define a complete line-oriented structural grammar and deterministic
      recovery matrix while preserving blank-line-delimited prose and attached
      literal blocks as source-ranged semantic units.

      Scope: Governs parsing and recovered document representation; excludes
      host-assisted semantic judgment.

      Trade-offs: Structural recognition is intentionally line-oriented, recovery
      does not guess skipped declarations, and older sources require review when
      adjacent lines carried separate intent.

      Discarded alternatives: A loose text parser would lose structural guarantees,
      while a rigid body grammar would over-formalize authoring.

      Consequences: Independent parsers can reproduce valid structure, diagnostic
      ordering, retained partial content, and recovery boundaries.

      Revisit when: Body tolerance prevents reliable projections or authoring
      evidence supports additional syntax.
    }

    StrictImportUse {
      Decision: Reject each resolved imported name that has no qualifying contract,
      operational, structural, or directory-surface use in its declaring source.

      Scope: Governs named component imports and imported public-concept reuse;
      excludes unresolved names and documentary mentions in goal, decisions, literal
      bodies, and other source documents.

      Trade-offs: Authors must state real dependency use outside documentary
      rationale, while provider directory-surface imports remain intentionally
      usable.

      Design issues addressed: Resolution previously accepted dependency declarations
      that had no effect on the importing contract or operational description.

      Discarded alternatives: Counting every textual mention or treating the import
      declaration itself as sufficient use was rejected.

      Consequences: Check reports one stable error per unused imported name and
      dependency graphs no longer accept accidental resolved imports silently.
    }

    ExplicitCrossFileExpansion {
      Decision: Require every cross-file expand to import its matching component
      explicitly.

      Scope: Governs expansion matching, collection, concept namespaces, and
      structural import use; excludes expands colocated with their component.

      Trade-offs: Expansion files require explicit dependency declarations, while
      resolution no longer depends on workspace-global name matching.

      Discarded alternatives: Globally matching expands by component name was
      rejected because it hides dependency ownership and permits accidental expansion
      attachment.

      Consequences: Unimported cross-file expands produce
      SIGIL_EXPAND_WITHOUT_COMPONENT, imported expands count as structural use, and
      unrelated imported names remain subject to SIGIL_UNUSED_IMPORT.
    }

    SemanticParagraphs {
      Decision: Make each blank-line-delimited prose paragraph one semantic unit
      whose physical wrapping may change without changing identity.

      Scope: Governs free-form content in every component and expand section;
      excludes structural forms and attached literal-body content.

      Trade-offs: Adjacent physical lines merge semantically and migration from
      line-based interpretation requires review.

      Design issues addressed: A deterministic formatter cannot wrap prose safely
      when every physical line creates a different semantic unit.

      Discarded alternatives: Preserving physical-line identity and adding
      continuation punctuation were rejected.

      Consequences: Formatting can reflow prose while blank lines remain the explicit
      separator between ideas.
    }

    LiteralFencedBlocks {
      Decision: Attach a Markdown-shaped typed backtick fence directly to preceding
      prose and preserve its body as uninterpreted literal content.

      Scope: Governs examples, configuration, code, data, diagrams, and other
      multiline literal content inside sections and concept blocks.

      Trade-offs: Standalone or blank-line-detached fences are invalid and literal
      content is unavailable to semantic reference and glossary scanning.

      Design issues addressed: Multiline code and data currently collide with brace
      tracking, semantic-unit separation, and line-width formatting.

      Discarded alternatives: Treating each literal line as prose, interpreting
      literal braces, and inventing format-specific section syntax were rejected.

      Consequences: Authors can embed large typed blocks without changing semantic
      separators or creating accidental references.
    }

    DeterministicFormatting {
      Decision: Canonically wrap ordinary prose to 79 content characters excluding
      leading indentation while preserving semantic identity and literal bodies.

      Scope: Governs physical presentation of Sigil prose; excludes structural lines,
      fence delimiters, and literal bodies.

      Trade-offs: An indivisible token longer than the limit remains unchanged,
      produces error SIGIL_UNFORMATTABLE_LINE over its complete physical line, and
      prevents a writable formatting result.

      Design issues addressed: Unbounded prose lines reduce side-by-side review
      readability and discourage concrete configuration and code examples.

      Consequences: Deterministic formatting is reviewable, idempotent, and
      independent from nesting depth.
    }

    ConceptIdentity {
      Decision: Use one flat collective concept namespace per component context and
      allow imported identity reuse only through matching expands.

      Scope: Governs concept identity within a component, its matching expands, and
      accessible imported public concepts; excludes glossary identity and source
      anchors.

      Trade-offs: Component declarations cannot reuse imported concept blocks;
      same-named local and imported identities require renaming, while contradictory
      occurrences remain semantic-review issues.

      Discarded alternatives: Nested qualification, global consumer merging, local
      shadowing, and later-block override were rejected.

      Consequences: Matching-expand reuse preserves the provider identity and every
      contextual occurrence, while component-declared collisions remain explicit and
      provider context remains stable as consumers change.

      Revisit when: Multi-hop re-exposure becomes unclear or collective occurrences
      cannot be reviewed reliably.
    }

    ImportResolution {
      Decision: Resolve every import from the selected workspace root through one
      lexical normalization and exact file-or-provider-directory selection rule.

      Scope: Governs import target identity and workspace containment; excludes
      workspace discovery and operating-system symbolic-link identity.

      Trade-offs: Source-relative imports are unavailable and traversal outside the
      selected workspace remains unresolved even when a filesystem target exists.

      Discarded alternatives: Declaring-source-relative lookup, implicit extension
      search, platform-dependent case folding, and multiple candidate targets were
      rejected.

      Consequences: Equivalent lexical paths produce one deterministic dependency
      edge and directory imports reuse their provider-owned surface.
    }

    DeclarationMultiplicity {
      Decision: Require workspace-unique component names, allow repeated sections as
      ordered collective contributions, and preserve repeated import-name entries.

      Scope: Governs declaration collisions and repetition; excludes concept
      identity, which follows its own collective namespace rules.

      Trade-offs: Duplicate components cannot participate in resolution until
      renamed, while repeated import entries may produce separate use diagnostics.

      Discarded alternatives: First- or last-declaration wins, duplicate-section
      errors, and silent import-entry deduplication were rejected.

      Consequences: No source-order winner hides a component collision, and
      intentional section composition retains source ordering.
    }

    StateData {
      Decision: Let state describe meaningful runtime or domain data alongside
      configurations, modes, and conditions.

      Scope: Governs data that exists or changes as part of component semantics;
      excludes implementation storage layout unless that layout carries domain
      meaning.

      Trade-offs: Authors must distinguish meaningful data from transient algorithm
      variables and persistence mechanics.

      Design issues addressed: Treating state as modes and conditions alone omits
      data-bearing configurations that implementations and dependents must
      understand.

      Discarded alternatives: Putting all data in interface or treating every stored
      field as state was rejected because visibility and storage do not determine
      semantic ownership.

      Consequences: State can capture data values and structures that materially
      shape behavior without exposing database or memory layout by default.
    }

    SectionSemantics {
      Decision: Keep decisions optional and free-form while using them for concise
      human-reviewed rationale separate from binding constraints.

      Scope: Governs decisions sections in expand declarations; excludes generated
      receipts, approval records, and binding authority.

      Trade-offs: Deterministic tools cannot guarantee that every rationale record is
      complete.

      Discarded alternatives: Constraints-only rationale, mandatory decision schemas,
      raw transcripts, and generated receipts as the sole record were rejected.

      Consequences: Binding outcomes remain in constraints and their rationale stays
      colocated with the governing component.

      Revisit when: Free-form records drift or reliable agent use requires a reviewed
      schema.
    }
  }

  cases {
    LanguageVersion {
      A tool that does not support the configured LanguageVersion reports an error
      before interpreting workspace Sigil files.

      A document configured for an earlier or later non-exact version is not silently
      reinterpreted using 0.7.0 semantics.
    }

    SourceDocument {
      A malformed top-level or form-body line is diagnosed, skipped, and followed by
      deterministic interpretation at the same structural level.

      An EOF-unclosed concept, section, component, or expand is finalized at EOF with
      source-ranged diagnostics and retained recoverable content.

      An unsupported LanguageVersion returns an empty document before declaration
      interpretation.

      A malformed but recoverable component retains its declarations and semantic
      units with source-ranged diagnostics.

      In `A😀B` on line two, B begins at source column four and LSP character three;
      the end of a CRLF-terminated line excludes both newline code units.

      A contiguous ungrouped interface region produces one
      SIGIL_MISSING_CONCEPT_IDENTIFIER warning.

      Two free interface paragraphs separated by a blank line remain retained and
      produce two warnings, each spanning only its paragraph's semantic unit.

      An empty or nested concept block is invalid.

      Wrapped prose with no blank separator produces one semantic unit.

      Introducing prose immediately followed by a typed fence produces one semantic
      unit with one attached literal block.

      Four leading backticks followed by json use a four-backtick opening delimiter
      and the supplied literal type json.

      A blank-line-detached, unintroduced, invalidly typed, or unclosed fence is
      invalid.

      Two contiguous unintroduced fences produce two empty-prose semantic units, each
      ranged over and attached to its own retained literal.

      Multiple prose braces on one line contribute their net left-to-right depth
      change; braces in a literal contribute none.

      An unmatched prose closing brace lowers depth, prevents a later structural
      close until balancing prose opens restore the baseline, and at EOF retains
      content under the applicable unclosed-structure recovery.

      An unexpected line is ranged over that line, while an EOF-unclosed component is
      ranged at the zero-width start of its component header.
    }

    ComponentContract {
      A component can be understood through its goal and interface without reading
      its expands or implementation.

      Multiple matching expands remain separately sourced contributions to one
      collected operational description.
    }

    ConceptIdentity {
      Reusing one public concept in interface, state, logic, constraints, decisions,
      or cases preserves one collective identity.

      Importing two different public concepts whose identifiers compare equal without
      regard to case produces one SIGIL_AMBIGUOUS_CONCEPT_IDENTIFIER error over the
      consumer component; both identities remain accessible and occurrences remain
      unbound.

      A concept introduced only outside interface remains private.

      Reusing an imported public concept in a matching expand preserves its
      originating identity without changing provider context.

      Declaring the same identifier on the importing component produces an
      ambiguous-concept diagnostic.

      PublicConcept resolves in `(PublicConcept)` and next to non-ASCII letters, but
      not in `APublicConcept`, `PublicConcept2`, `PublicConcept_name`, or
      `PublicConcept-name`.
    }

    ImportSemantics {
      Importing Name makes Name's public contract available without exposing its
      collected expansions.

      Components named Name and name are distinct; importing Name or declaring expand
      Name never resolves or attaches name.

      A cross-file expand without an import produces SIGIL_EXPAND_WITHOUT_COMPONENT.

      Importing the component attaches the cross-file expand and records
      structural-expand use.

      A second unused name in the same import still produces SIGIL_UNUSED_IMPORT.

      A structural self-import does not duplicate the component's public concepts.

      A missing source retains an import result with no resolved names; a missing
      component or directory-import name retains every independently resolved name
      from the same import.

      Missing paths are diagnosed before missing names, and a cycle diagnostic is
      anchored to the import edge that returns to an active source.

      An import referenced only in goal or decisions produces SIGIL_UNUSED_IMPORT.

      An exact component reference in constraints uses its import.

      ImportedComponent resolves in `(ImportedComponent)` and next to non-ASCII
      letters, but not in `AnImportedComponent`, `ImportedComponent2`,
      `ImportedComponent_name`, or `ImportedComponent-name`.

      An imported public concept reused in logic uses its provider import.

      A provider name occurring only inside a literal block does not use its import.

      A provider name in comment-shaped ordinary constraints prose uses its import.

      `@a/./b/../c` and `@a/c` select the same workspace-root-relative directory
      target; `@../a` and `@a/../../b` retain outside traversal and are unresolved.

      A repeated imported name remains separately ordered and checked for use without
      duplicating its provider concept identity.

      One textual or structural use qualifies every repeated entry for the same name
      and provider; with no use, every entry receives SIGIL_UNUSED_IMPORT.

      A resolved direct import exposed by a provider directory surface counts as
      structural use.
    }

    DeclarationMultiplicity {
      Two components with the same name are both retained and diagnosed, and neither
      is selected by an import or matching expand.

      Two component names differing only by case are not duplicates.

      Two logic sections contribute their semantic units in source order.
    }

    SectionSemantics {
      Goal and interface together make a component understandable to dependents.

      State may describe a domain value that changes behavior without exposing its
      database or memory representation.

      An expand may omit decisions without losing validity.

      A decisions section may contain free prose or named concept blocks with
      rationale labels.

      A binding stack choice appears in constraints while a corresponding decisions
      entry explains its alternatives and trade-offs.
    }

    FormattingStyle {
      Rewrapping one prose semantic unit at 79 content characters preserves its
      semantic identity.

      Six leading spaces do not reduce the 79-character prose-content allowance.

      A literal body remains byte-faithful after structural dedentation and is not
      wrapped.

      An indivisible prose token longer than 79 content characters produces
      SIGIL_UNFORMATTABLE_LINE, remains in the partial document, prevents a writable
      formatting result, and makes check fail.
    }
  }
}

component SigilWorkspaceConfig {
  goal {
    Define the configuration contract that selects and identifies one Sigil
    workspace.

    Keep workspace boundaries and source discovery independent from package
    manifests and directory-import indexes.
  }

  interface {
    ConfigFile {
      filename: .sigil/config.json

      location: workspace root
    }

    ConfiguredLanguageVersion {
      sigilVersion: the supported Sigil language version used to interpret the
      workspace
    }

    WorkspaceBoundary {
      workspace.name: non-empty stable workspace identifier

      workspace.members: optional array of unique workspace-relative project-root
      paths
    }

    SourceSelection {
      files.include: non-empty array of workspace-relative POSIX glob strings

      files.exclude?: array of workspace-relative POSIX glob strings
    }

    ToolConfiguration {
      tools?: object of namespaced JSON objects preserved for host integrations
    }
  }
}

expand SigilWorkspaceConfig {
  constraints {
    ConfigFile {
      ConfigFile is the sole authority for the Sigil workspace boundary.

      The directory containing ConfigFile is the workspace root and root project
      location.
    }

    ConfiguredLanguageVersion {
      The configured sigilVersion must equal a language version supported by the
      interpreting tool.
    }

    WorkspaceBoundary {
      Each workspace.members entry declares one additional project root in the same
      workspace.

      Member paths are unique, non-root, workspace-relative directories that cannot
      escape the workspace or overlap.

      A declared member does not contain its own ConfigFile.

      A nested directory with its own ConfigFile is an independent workspace and must
      be excluded from every configured parent.

      Package and repository workspace manifests do not declare Sigil workspace
      members.
    }

    SourceSelection {
      SourceSelection controls source discovery but does not declare workspace
      members or restrict ModuleIndexFile locations.
    }

    ModuleIndexFile {
      ModuleIndexFile has no workspace-discovery or membership authority.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    WorkspaceBoundary {
      Decision: Make ConfigFile the sole authority for workspace roots and declared
      members.

      Scope: Governs the workspace root, declared members, and independent nested
      workspaces; excludes ModuleIndexFile and GlossaryFile authority.

      Trade-offs: Repositories maintain explicit Sigil configuration in addition to
      package or build manifests.

      Discarded alternatives: Inferring boundaries from package manifests or
      directory structure was rejected because those sources vary by host and
      purpose.

      Consequences: Nested independent workspaces must be excluded explicitly and
      package metadata cannot silently change Sigil membership.

      Revisit when: Another explicit interoperable authority can replace ConfigFile
      without introducing boundary ambiguity.
    }
  }
}

component SigilModuleIndex {
  goal {
    Define the explicit directory-import index for an included Sigil directory.

    Provide intentional directory-import shorthand without changing component
    visibility or workspace discovery.
  }

  interface {
    ModuleIndexFile {
      filename: _module.sigil

      location: any workspace directory where _module.sigil itself is selected and
      loaded by ConfigFile source selection
    }

    DirectoryImportSurface {
      a directory import @path resolves through @path/_module.sigil

      includes components declared directly in ModuleIndexFile

      includes components named by direct imports in ModuleIndexFile
    }

    ComponentVisibility {
      every component remains importable through its explicit .sigil source path
      regardless of ModuleIndexFile
    }

    ModuleIndexContents {
      contains at least one local component declaration

      may additionally contain ordinary imports and expands
    }
  }
}

expand SigilModuleIndex {
  logic {
    DirectoryImportSurface {
      Resolve each direct import in ModuleIndexFile using ordinary import rules.

      Build the directory-import surface from local component declarations and
      successfully resolved directly imported names.

      Preserve each resolved component's original declaration file and collected
      expansions.

      Resolve a named directory import against the resulting surface.
    }
  }

  constraints {
    ModuleIndexFile {
      ModuleIndexFile defines directory-import shorthand only.

      ModuleIndexFile does not define a workspace root, workspace member, or special
      component type.
    }

    ComponentVisibility {
      ModuleIndexFile does not grant or restrict component visibility.

      A component omitted from ModuleIndexFile remains importable through its
      explicit source path.

      Sigil defines no export or re-export form.
    }

    DirectoryImportSurface {
      Components used only as dependencies of indexed files are not added to the
      surface implicitly.

      Files and components elsewhere in the directory or subtree are not added
      implicitly.

      An explicit chain of module-index imports may make a component resolvable
      through multiple directory indexes.

      Repeated surface contributions for the same component declaration identity
      deduplicate. Distinct declarations cannot share an exact component name because
      duplicated names remain unresolved; local and imported contributions have no
      precedence.

      An unresolved direct-import path contributes no names. A direct import with one
      or more unresolved names still contributes every independently resolved name
      from that declaration. Neither failure invalidates local names or names from
      other independently resolved direct imports.

      Resolve chained and cyclic surfaces to the least fixed point. For each name,
      monotonically accumulate the set of distinct declaration identities contributed
      across all reached indexes. Seed those sets with locally declared components
      and independently resolved direct-file imports, then visit module-index imports
      in source and declaration order until no identity set grows. After convergence,
      expose a name only when its set contains exactly one identity; expose no name
      for an empty or multiple-identity set. A cycle never synthesizes an unseeded
      name.
    }

    ModuleIndexContents {
      Every ModuleIndexFile contains at least one local component declaration.

      SIGIL_MODULE_WITHOUT_COMPONENT does not invalidate independently resolved
      imported names in the partial directory-import surface.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    ModuleIndexFile {
      Decision: Reserve _module.sigil as the sole ModuleIndexFile basename and do not
      interpret the legacy hash-prefixed module filename as a directory index.

      Scope: Governs directory-index filename recognition and directory-import target
      selection; excludes ordinary explicit .sigil file imports.

      Trade-offs: Existing workspaces must rename every module index and update
      direct source references before adopting this language version.

      Design issues addressed: _module.sigil uses only the POSIX portable filename
      character set and remains a literal shell word without quoting or escaping.

      Discarded alternatives: Retaining the hash-prefixed filename with mandatory
      shell quoting and accepting both basenames indefinitely were rejected.

      Consequences: Directory imports resolve only through _module.sigil; the legacy
      filename is interpreted as an ordinary Sigil source.

      Revisit when: A future module-discovery mechanism replaces reserved index
      filenames.
    }

    DirectoryImportSurface {
      Decision: Use ModuleIndexFile as an explicit directory-import shorthand that
      requires a local component, never controls component visibility, and retains
      the partial surface available after independently recoverable diagnostics.

      Scope: Governs directory-import resolution for included directories; excludes
      workspace discovery and export visibility.

      Trade-offs: Index authors maintain an intentional surface and cannot use
      imports-only indexes. A diagnostically invalid index may still expose names
      whose declarations resolved independently.

      Discarded alternatives: Root-only indexes, implicit subtree exports,
      module-index-controlled visibility, and all-or-nothing invalidation after one
      import or missing-local-component diagnostic were rejected.

      Consequences: Components omitted from an index remain accessible only through
      explicit source imports. Callers may use unaffected names while diagnostics
      continue to report every unresolved path, name, or required local component.

      Revisit when: Directory imports require explicit export or visibility
      semantics.
    }
  }

  cases {
    DirectoryImportSurface {
      @auth import { Session } resolves when auth/_module.sigil declares Session or
      directly imports Session from another Sigil source.

      @auth import { Session } is unresolved when auth/_module.sigil does not name
      Session, while @auth/session.sigil import { Session } remains valid.

      @auth import { Store } is unresolved when Store is only a dependency of an
      indexed component.

      A directory without ModuleIndexFile produces SIGIL_UNRESOLVED_IMPORT_PATH.

      Two mutually importing ModuleIndexFiles propagate locally declared and
      direct-file-imported names around the cycle until the least fixed point. A name
      with no such seed remains unresolved. The cycle produces SIGIL_IMPORT_CYCLE and
      does not prevent declarations outside the cycle from resolving.

      Repeated direct imports that expose the same component declaration contribute
      that declaration once to the directory-import surface.

      Distinct declarations with the same exact component name remain unresolved and
      produce SIGIL_DUPLICATE_COMPONENT; that name is absent from the surface while
      unaffected names remain available.

      If an index first contributes one Session identity and a cyclic index later
      contributes a distinct Session identity, convergence retains both identities,
      removes Session from the exposed surfaces, and produces
      SIGIL_DUPLICATE_COMPONENT.

      If one name in a multi-name direct import is unresolved, every independently
      resolved name from that declaration remains available through the partial
      surface and the unresolved-name diagnostic remains on ModuleIndexFile.

      An unresolved direct-import path contributes no names, while local components
      and names from other resolved direct imports remain available.

      A directory importer that requests a name absent from that partial surface
      receives SIGIL_UNRESOLVED_IMPORTED_COMPONENT.

      When auth/_module.sigil exists but ConfigFile source selection excludes it,
      @auth import { Session } produces SIGIL_UNRESOLVED_IMPORT_PATH.
    }

    ModuleIndexContents {
      An imports-only ModuleIndexFile produces SIGIL_MODULE_WITHOUT_COMPONENT while
      an independently resolved imported name remains available through the partial
      surface. A directory importer requesting any other name receives
      SIGIL_UNRESOLVED_IMPORTED_COMPONENT.
    }
  }
}
